= Salience — Technical =

== Priority maps ==
Computational attention models can combine feature contrast into a priority map:

<pre>
priority(x) = f(bottom-up contrast,
                task goals,
                learned value,
                selection history)
</pre>

Purely stimulus-driven salience is therefore only one component of broader priority.

== Incentive salience ==
In motivational neuroscience, incentive salience refers to learned "wanting"-like motivational value assigned to reward-predictive cues.

It is distinct from sensory salience and from hedonic pleasure.

== Neural salience ==
The phrase "salience network" refers to a particular large-scale neural-network literature.

That network label should not be treated as the definition of psychological salience itself.

== Measurement ==
Eye movements, detection probability, response time, neural responses, choice, and subjective ratings operationalize different forms of salience.

